'''New Burawa''' (Burawi: {{lang|so|Burawi Cusub}}), commonly referred to as '''Cusub''' in both Burawi and English usage, refers to the totality of [[Kingdom of Burawa|Burawi]] territorial possessions on the eastern seaboard of [[Abyala]]. The colony was established in 1516 with the founding of [[Lacashe]] and persisted through the colonial expansion period until the [[Burawi Imperial Split of 1709]], when the Burawi state divided into the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] (east) and the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]] (west).

New Burawa comprised three provinces stretching from southw to north along the eastern coast of Abyala, east of the [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range]]: the southern province of '''[[Lacashe]]''', the middle province of '''[[Bindorama]]''', and the northern province of '''[[Arakwaya]]'''. The territory corresponds to portions of the modern countries of [[Asikyira]] and [[Balisca]].

== Etymology ==

The term '''Cusub''' derives from the [[Burawi language|Burawi]] word for "new" or "newly established," reflecting the colony's status as an overseas territorial possession distinct from the metropolitan peninsula. The full designation '''Burawi Cusub''' translates literally as "New Burawa" and was used to distinguish the Abyalan territories from the ancestral homeland on the Kaftian peninsula.

The names of the three provinces reflect their origins: '''Lacashe''' is a Burawi name given to the original landing site; '''Bindorama''' derives from the Burawi corruption of the Aruanãyára (Tupi) word ''Pindorama'', meaning "palm trees"; and '''Arakwaya''' derives from the Burawi corruption of the Aruanãyára word ''Araguaia'', a river name.

== Geography ==

[[File:New_Burawa_map_1620.png|thumb|300px|Map of New Burawa circa 1620, showing the three provinces along the eastern Abyalan coast]]

New Burawa occupied a coastal strip along the eastern seaboard of [[Abyala]], extending from the southern landing site at [[Lacashe]] northward along the coast. The territory was bounded to the west by the [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range]], the mountain chain that ran along Abyala's eastern seaboard.

The three provinces differed significantly in geography and character:

=== Lacashe (Southern Province) ===

[[File:Lacashe_harbour_1600.jpg|thumb|250px|The harbour of Lacashe, the colony's primary port and administrative centre, circa 1600]]

The southernmost province of '''Lacashe''' comprised mostly hills, plains, lush flatlands, and marshes. The relatively flat terrain and fertile soils made it ideal for plantation agriculture. The province contained the most extensive agricultural land in New Burawa and was home to the largest slave ports and plantation complexes. The climate was tropical, with high temperatures and seasonal rainfall supporting year-round cultivation.

The province was the most densely settled and the most firmly under Burawi administrative control. The capital city of [[Lacashe]] served as the colony's primary harbour, the seat of the colonial bishopric, and the residence of the senior royal governor. Plantation estates lined the coastal lowlands, worked by enslaved Kaftian labourers transported across the [[Aethiopean Ocean]]. The province's wealth derived from sugar, tobacco, and cotton cultivation, as well as from the export of gold extracted from the foothills to the north.

=== Bindorama (Middle Province) ===

[[File:Bindorama_valley_1650.jpg|thumb|250px|Mountain valley settlements in Bindorama, where indigenous populations maintained urbanised communities]]

The middle province of '''Bindorama''' occupied a coastal strip with a narrow coastal plain that quickly gave way to the rising [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range]]. While all three provinces bordered the Aethiopean Ocean, Bindorama's usable coastal lowlands were more limited than those of Lacashe, and the terrain transitioned rapidly into hills, valleys, and mountain foothills. This province was the smallest of the three but contained the most urbanized indigenous populations, who had established settlements in the mountain valleys prior to Burawi conquest.

The province was known to Baliscano and Volisanian peoples as '''La Frontera''' ("the frontier"), reflecting its character as a transition zone between the "wild north" and the "colonial south." Indigenous communities in Bindorama maintained a degree of autonomy unusual in the colonial context, negotiating with colonial governors and preserving elements of their pre-conquest social organisation. The provincial capital of [[Gulega]] was named after the colony's founder, [[Guled Badweyn]], and served as an administrative centre for the mountain valley regions.

==== Dakari migration ====

Unlike Lacashe, which drew settlers primarily from the Burawi heartland and Mesogean coastal regions, Bindorama received the majority of its colonial-era migrants from the northern regions of mainland Burawa, particularly from [[Dakare]] and surrounding areas. These '''Dakari''' settlers spoke '''Waajuri''', a language primarily associated with the northern territories of the Burawi kingdom, and brought with them distinct cultural practices, agricultural techniques adapted to hill terrain, and kinship networks that shaped the province's settlement patterns.

The Dakari were drawn to Bindorama for reasons distinct from the environmental similarities that motivated other migrant groups. The province's mountainous and rugged terrain was less appealing for the large plantation agriculture that dominated Lacashe, making it unattractive to the slave-owning elite and central authorities. This relative neglect by the colonial administration offered the Dakari greater autonomy from Burawi rule, including more freedom to practice their distinct religious traditions without interference from the [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]] authorities who held greater sway in the more controlled southern province. Additionally, the crown offered land grants in Bindorama as an incentive for settlement in a province that Burawi authorities struggled to control effectively. Many Dakari settlers established smallholdings in the mountain valleys, engaging in mixed agriculture and small-scale mining rather than the plantation monoculture that dominated Lacashe.

Over time, the Dakari population of Bindorama developed a distinct colonial dialect influenced by contact with indigenous Aruanãyára languages and, to a lesser extent, Volisanian traders along the mountain frontier. This linguistic heritage persisted in the region long after the colonial period ended, contributing to the distinctive cultural character of modern [[Asikyira]], where Dakari-derived place names and agricultural practices remain evident.

=== Arakwaya (Northern Province) ===

[[File:Hesperian_Coast_1650.jpg|thumb|250px|Trading posts along the Hesperian Coast, the multi-ethnic northern frontier of New Burawa]]

The northernmost province of '''Arakwaya''' was the largest by area but the least controlled by Burawi authorities. The province comprised mostly wilderness, with scattered trading posts and small settlements along the coast. The interior remained largely unexplored and unadministered, inhabited by indigenous peoples who maintained varying degrees of independence from colonial authority. The region became known as the '''Hesperian Coast''' for its multi-ethnic character and reputation as a destination for fortune-seekers from across the world.

Stories of gold discoveries in the northern wilderness attracted settlers, traders, and adventurers from the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] world, [[Illypnia]], and beyond. The provincial capital of [[New Samara]], named after the peninsular capital, began as a trading post and grew into a cosmopolitan port where Burawi, Volisanian, Illypnian, and indigenous peoples interacted with minimal colonial oversight. By 1610, the province had effectively detached from Burawi administration, operating as the semi-independent '''Hesperian League'''.

== History ==

=== Founding (1516) ===

The colony was established by the navigator [[Guled Badweyn]] in 1516, when he founded the settlement of [[Lacashe]] on the eastern coast of [[Abyala]]. The initial settlement comprised a fortified trading post, a harbour, a [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]] mission, and supporting agricultural land.

The site was chosen for its strategic position at the margin of two indigenous political systems: the [[Tapuya Confederacy]] to the west in the interior lowlands, and the [[Aruanãyára]] highland empire further north along the mountain range. Neither claimed direct sovereignty over the coast, facilitating the initial Burawi settlement.

=== Early settlement (1516–1530s) ===

The settler population of Lacashe was heterogeneous in composition. Soldiers garrisoned the fortifications and provided security against indigenous incursions. Farmers cleared and cultivated land for plantation agriculture, initially producing crops for local consumption and subsequently for export. Merchants established trading houses linked to the commercial networks of [[Mazar]] and [[Samara]]. [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]] missionaries operated parishes and schools. The crown also transported convicts to the colony, where they served sentences of forced labour before receiving land grants upon completion of their terms.

Relations with the indigenous coastal peoples were initially commercial rather than military. The settlers traded manufactured goods — iron tools, textiles, and glass — for food, knowledge of the terrain, and access to interior trade routes.

=== Expansion under Faarax III (1524–1563) ===

Under [[Faarax III]] (r. 1524–1563), the Burawi colonial presence expanded from the original settlement at [[Lacashe]] northward along the coast. The collective term '''Burawi Cusub''' came into use during this period to describe the totality of Burawi territorial possessions on the eastern seaboard of Abyala.

Colonial administration was organised through royal governors appointed from [[Samara]], supported by a civil bureaucracy modelled on the peninsular provincial system. The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] operated missions and parishes in parallel, and the [[Cardinal of Burawa]] appointed a bishop for the colonial territories. Towns were founded at harbour sites and river mouths along the coast, connected by a coastal road that facilitated both trade and military movement.

=== The Abyala Charter (1537) ===

{{main|Abyala Charter}}

In 1537, [[Kingdom of Burawa|Burawa]] and [[Volisanians|Volisania]] concluded the '''[[Abyala Charter]]''', a bilateral treaty that divided the continent between them. The [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range]] served as the principal boundary: territories east of the mountains were assigned to Burawa, territories west to Volisania. The charter established exclusive trade zones and mutual recognition of existing settlements.

=== Conquest of indigenous polities (1560s–1570s) ===

In the 1560s, during the reign of [[Iacob IV]], Burawi forces jointly conquered the [[Tapuya Confederacy]] with Volisanian allies. The Tapuya were a semi-nomadic people who inhabited the vast plains and river valleys of central Abyala. Their economy was based on seasonal agriculture, hunting, and the management of extensive trade networks. The confederacy's decentralised political structure proved a vulnerability: individual chiefdoms could be isolated and defeated without coordinated resistance.

The [[Aruanãyára]] highland empire was conquered by Burawi forces alone in the 1570s. The Aruanãyára possessed a more centralised political structure, with a ruling dynasty seated in a capital of wooden construction in the highland interior. The conquest required sustained military campaigns in mountainous terrain over a period of several years. The fall of the Aruanãyára extended Burawi control along the full length of the eastern mountain range and secured the colonial interior.

=== Aftermath for indigenous peoples ===

The conquests of the 1560s and 1570s were devastating for the indigenous populations of eastern [[Abyala]]. The [[Tapuya Confederacy|Tapuya]] chiefdoms, deprived of political autonomy and subjected to colonial administration, experienced rapid demographic decline. The semi-nomadic way of life that had sustained the Tapuya was incompatible with the fixed territorial boundaries imposed by the colonial regime, and the seizure of lands for plantation agriculture displaced communities from their seasonal circuits.

The [[Aruanãyára]] experienced a comparable collapse. The fall of the ruling dynasty and the dismantling of the imperial administrative hierarchy removed the institutions that had organised highland society. By the early 17th century, the indigenous peoples of eastern Abyala had been reduced to a fraction of their pre-contact population.

=== War of the Mesogean (1590s–1610s) ===

{{main|War of the Mesogean}}

The [[War of the Mesogean]] (~1590s–1610s) resulted in Burawi defeat and the loss of [[Kingdom of Reige|Reige]]. While New Burawa itself was not directly affected by the war, the loss of Reige deprived Burawa of its strategic foothold in the central [[Mesogean Sea]] and fundamentally altered the kingdom's colonial ambitions.

In the northern province of Arakwaya, the war's aftermath led to a significant reduction in Burawi influence. The province, already loosely controlled, effectively became autonomous under the name '''Hesperian League''' by approximately 1610. This multi-ethnic polity, centred on the trading post of [[New Samara]], attracted fortune-seekers from across the world with stories of gold and opportunity in the northern wilderness.

=== Late colonial period (1608–1709) ===

Following the War of the Mesogean, the colonial administration focused on consolidating existing territories rather than further expansion. The three provinces developed distinct trajectories. '''Lacashe''' remained the most firmly controlled province, with extensive plantation agriculture, slave ports, and close administrative ties to the metropolitan government. '''Bindorama''', known to Baliscano and Volisanian peoples as '''La Frontera''' ("the frontier"), served as a transition zone between the "wild north" and the "colonial south." The province maintained the most urbanised indigenous populations and a distinctive mixed culture. '''Arakwaya''' operated largely independently as the Hesperian League, with minimal Burawi administrative presence despite nominal claims of sovereignty.

=== End of the colony (1709) ===

{{main|1709 Split}}

The colony of New Burawa effectively ended with the [[1709 Split]], when the Burawi state divided into the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] (east) and the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]] (west). The division was the culmination of decades of tension between the [[Kadim-Galwa]] and [[Kadim-Kaysoor]] factions of the Burawi royal family.

The three provinces were divided between the successor states. '''Lacashe''' remained with the [[Kingdom of Burawa]], maintaining close ties to the peninsular homeland. '''Bindorama''' was assigned to the [[Burawi Kingdom of Galwa]], provoking resistance from Burawi loyalists who allied with indigenous peoples in a bloody civil war. This conflict led to the creation of '''[[Asikyira]]''', an independent state with a mixed Burawi-indigenous character. '''Arakwaya''' (the [[Hesperian League]]) had already been effectively independent since 1610 and formalised its status following the split.

