== Economy ==

[[File:Lacashe_plantation_1650.jpg|thumb|250px|Sugar plantation in Lacashe province, worked by enslaved Kaftian labourers]]

The economy of New Burawa was driven by three principal sectors:

=== Plantation agriculture ===

The fertile coastal lowlands were cultivated with plantation crops, primarily sugar, tobacco, and cotton, though the nature of plantation agriculture differed significantly between the provinces. In '''Lacashe''', the colony's wealthiest and most controlled province, plantations were worked by enslaved Kaftian labour under a system of chattel slavery. Large estates concentrated in the flatlands surrounding the capital produced sugar, the colony's most valuable export, which was refined in colonial mills and shipped to Mesogean markets. Tobacco and cotton were secondary crops grown on smaller estates.

In '''Bindorama''', coastal plantations were more limited in scale compared to those of Lacashe. The province's plantation economy relied primarily upon indentured servitude rather than chattel slavery. The labour force comprised '''Dakari''' migrants from northern Burawa and '''Ayori''' peoples from the neighbouring mainland, who contracted to work the land for fixed terms, typically ten years, after which they received their freedom and, in many cases, land grants. This system produced sugar, tobacco, and cotton for export, but on a smaller scale than the slave-worked estates of the southern province.

=== Gold extraction ===

[[File:Arakwaya_gold_rush_1600.jpg|thumb|250px|Gold prospectors in the Arakwaya wilderness, circa 1600]]

Alluvial gold deposits in the foothills of the [[Matarrañya-Cañadapirén Range]], primarily in Bindorama and southern Arakwaya, were extracted through both panning operations and more organised mining ventures. Gold was the colony's most valuable export and a major source of revenue for the crown through taxation and export duties. Stories of gold discoveries in the northern wilderness attracted fortune-seekers from across the world to the Hesperian Coast.

Gold extraction began in the Bindorama foothills during the mid-16th century, following the conquest of the Aruanãyára. The discovery of richer deposits further north in Arakwaya sparked a gold rush in the late 16th century, drawing prospectors from Burawa, Volisania, Illypnia, and beyond. The gold rush contributed to the multi-ethnic character of the Hesperian Coast and to the weakening of Burawi administrative control in the northern province.

=== Slave trade ===

The [[Kaftian slave trade]] was a central component of the colonial economy. Enslaved persons were captured or purchased in [[Kaftia]] — through agreements with rulers in [[Ayorin]], [[Imfezzans|Imfezzan]] intermediaries, and northern Kaftian peoples — and transported from the [[Burawi North Kaftia|North Kaftian]] coastal forts across the [[Aethiopean Ocean]] to the Abyalan colonies. The enslaved population included peoples from the Ayorin interior, the Tamazgha desert fringe, the northern Kaftian coastlands, and communities captured in raids.

The majority of enslaved persons were transported to Lacashe, where the plantation economy absorbed the largest share of the trade. By the late 17th century, the enslaved population constituted the majority of the province's inhabitants. The slave trade generated substantial revenue for the crown through export duties and licence fees, and the merchant houses of [[Mazar]] and [[Iskhal]] that controlled the trade became among the wealthiest in the kingdom.

=== Trade networks ===

New Burawa was integrated into a triangle trade connecting Kaftia, Abyala, and the Mesogean world. Enslaved persons were transported from Kaftia to the Abyalan colonies; colonial goods — gold, sugar, tobacco, cotton, and tropical products — were shipped to the Kaftian peninsula and Mesogean markets; and manufactured goods and trade items were exported from the peninsula to Kaftia, completing the cycle.

The colony also maintained direct trade with Volisanian Abyala, despite the trade restrictions of the [[Abyala Charter]]. Smuggling between Burawi and Volisanian colonies was widespread, particularly along the mountain frontier in Bindorama. The Hesperian Coast developed its own trade networks independent of both Burawi and Volisanian control, with ships calling at New Samara from ports across the Mesogean and Aethiopean worlds.

== Administration ==

New Burawa was administered through a system of royal governors appointed by the crown in [[Samara]]. Each governor exercised civil and military authority within a defined territorial district, subject to directives from the metropolitan government and periodic review by royal inspectors. The governor of [[Lacashe]], the oldest and most important district, held precedence over the other colonial governors and served as the crown's principal representative in Abyala.

Below the governors, a civil bureaucracy administered justice, collected taxes, and managed the allocation of land to settlers. The legal system in the colonies was an extension of peninsular Burawi law, though in practice the distance from the metropolitan courts and the discretionary authority granted to governors produced significant local variation.

The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] operated a parallel institutional structure. The colonial bishopric, created under [[Dalgayan III]], administered parishes, schools, and missions throughout the colonial territories.

Administrative control varied significantly across the three provinces. Lacashe was governed directly from Samara with close oversight; Bindorama operated with considerable local autonomy under governors who negotiated with indigenous elites; and Arakwaya was administered through a network of trading posts with minimal effective control beyond the immediate vicinity of coastal settlements.

== Demographics ==

The settler population of New Burawa was predominantly male and of mixed origin, comprising Burawi colonists, Kaftian migrants from the peninsula, and individuals of mixed Burawi-indigenous descent born in the colony. The indigenous population declined dramatically following contact due to disease, displacement, and enslavement, while the demographic void was filled by the importation of enslaved Kaftian labourers.

The colonial society was stratified along lines of race and status: white settlers at the top, mixed-race colonists in the middle, and enslaved persons at the bottom. The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] operated missions that sought to convert both indigenous peoples and enslaved persons, though conversion did not necessarily translate into social equality.

The demographic character varied by province. '''Lacashe''' had the highest proportion of enslaved persons, who constituted the majority of the population by the late 17th century. '''Bindorama''' maintained the largest free indigenous population, concentrated in urbanised settlements in the mountain valleys. '''Arakwaya''' was the most ethnically diverse, attracting settlers, traders, and fortune-seekers from across the Mesogean world, Illypnia, and beyond.

== Legacy ==

The colonial period left a lasting legacy on the region. The territories that comprised New Burawa correspond to portions of modern [[Asikyira]] and [[Balisca]]. The Burawi language, culture, and [[Burawi Catholic Church|Christianity]] were introduced during this period and persisted even after the colonial era ended.

The 1709 Split divided the former colony between the Kingdom of Burawa, the Burawi Kingdom of Galwa, and the independent Hesperian League. The civil war in Bindorama led to the creation of [[Asikyira]], a state with a distinctive mixed Burawi-indigenous character that persists to the present day.

The economic structures established during the colonial period — plantation agriculture, gold extraction, and the slave trade — shaped the region's development for centuries. The demographic changes brought about by the slave trade created multi-ethnic societies that distinguished the former New Burawa territories from other parts of Abyala.

